{:contract/kind :actor
 :actor/id "deploy_verifier"
 :actor/kind :agent
 ;; The verifier's own identity, deliberately not the bootstrap system
 ;; admin's. The :trusted-loopback grant in
 ;; contracts/knoxx/authentication/mcp_http.edn resolves its context by
 ;; :grant/user-email and law/token-actor-honourable? then requires that
 ;; membership's stored actor binding to be deploy_verifier — this email is
 ;; what makes the two the same membership. It is a dedicated one because the
 ;; role projection and the authentication grant independently clamp what the
 ;; long-lived loopback bearer can do; a small read-only surface is the point.
 :actor/email "deploy-verifier@open-hax.local"
 :actor/username "deploy-verifier@open-hax.local"
 :actor/org "open-hax"
 :actor/label "Deploy Verifier"
 ;; Exactly the probe role, and nothing else. Role projection replaces this
 ;; membership's roles with this list (:replace true in
 ;; project-actor-role-slugs!), which here touches only the verifier's own
 ;; membership — the reason the identity had to be dedicated rather than the
 ;; admin's.
 :actor/roles [:role/deploy-verifier]
 :actor/policy
 {:principal :actor
  :source-of-truth :contract
  :notes
  "The identity the post-deploy MCP gate runs as: two read-only tools via
   :role/deploy-verifier -> :cap/deploy-verify, and no more. The loopback
   token that reaches it is loopback-only and inert without the provisioned
   secret, and even presented correctly it cannot write, dispatch, spawn, or
   administer. Neither probe depends on an unrelated actor-owned external
   credential; that keeps MCP deployment health separate from Discord account
   provisioning."}}
